  "body": "## Finding Description and Impact\n\n`Covenant.multicall` sets a contract-wide `_isMulticall` guard to `true` before delegating into `MulticallLib.multicall` and unconditionally clears it to `false` once the delegatecall returns ([`src/Covenant.sol:516-519`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/main/src/Covenant.sol#L516-L519)). The library processes each payload item via `Address.functionDelegateCall(address(this), data)` ([`src/libraries/MultiCall.sol:21-24`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/main/src/libraries/MultiCall.sol#L21-L24)), which means an inner payload can invoke `Covenant.multicall` again. The nested invocation flips `_isMulticall` back to `true`, but when the nested batch unwinds it resets the flag to `false` while the outer batch is still iterating. Subsequent outer calls therefore execute with `_isMulticall == false` even though they are still part of the original multicall.\n\nPayment-sensitive entrypoints like `updateState`, `mint`, `redeem`, and `swap` rely on `_checkPayment(msgValue)` to skip `msg.value == msgValue` enforcement during batched execution ([`src/Covenant.sol:556-559`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/main/src/Covenant.sol#L556-L559)). Once the flag desynchronizes, the remaining batch items begin enforcing the equality check mid-flight and deterministically revert with `Errors.E_IncorrectPayment`. Any batch that nests `multicall`—even indirectly through helper calls—breaks for the remainder of the outer sequence. This is a reliable denial-of-service condition affecting multicall composability rather than a funds-draining bug.\n\n### Affected code\n- [`src/Covenant.sol:516-559`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/main/src/Covenant.sol#L516-L559)\n- [`src/libraries/MultiCall.sol:17-25`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/main/src/libraries/MultiCall.sol#L17-L25)\n\n```solidity\n// src/Covenant.sol\n_isMulticall = true;\nresults = MulticallLib.multicall(data);\n_isMulticall = false;\n\nfunction _checkPayment(uint256 msgValue) private {\n    if (msg.value != msgValue && !_isMulticall) revert Errors.E_IncorrectPayment();\n}\n```\n\n---\n## Impact\n\n- Nested multicalls cause outer batch items that follow the nested invocation to revert with `Errors.E_IncorrectPayment`, halting execution and wasting gas.\n- Integrations that rely on delegated batching (e.g., routers that compose protocol actions) can be griefed by inserting a nested `multicall`, turning otherwise valid sequences into guaranteed reverts.\n- Because the final balance check only triggers after the outer batch finishes, the DoS effect persists for the remainder of the transaction without any post-hoc recovery.\n\n---\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\n1. Replace the boolean guard with a reference counter that increments before entering `MulticallLib.multicall` and decrements afterwards, ensuring `_isMulticall` remains `true` until the outermost multicall finishes.\n2. Alternatively, prevent re-entrant calls to `multicall` entirely (e.g., `require(!_isMulticall)` before setting the flag) if nesting is not a supported use case.\n3. Consider moving the flag management into the library or using `try/finally`-style patterns to guarantee restoration even if future logic introduces early returns.\n\n---",
